(7339302, 1144176, 93565, '<td valign="top">First, head isn''t in a clean spot. Shifting your position slightly left and having him a bit more to the right would help showcase both him and the building. It looks like you missed focus a little bit, as well. Both the concrete stairs, as well as parts of him (especially foot) lack the clarity of the rest of the scene. This may be a situation to consider a hyperfocal setting, upping iso and stopping down another stop, or accepting the lack of clarity in the background, instead (see further down regarding that consideration). From an action sports photo perspective, you want to separate subject from background in a shot like this, but with that cool building, you''re in a tricky spot wherein you need to decide what type of an audience you''re after. Since this is a senior photo shoot, you''re aiming to focus on your model, so I''d say a decrease in DoF would be welcomed. For a photo on here, a deeper DoF will probably work better as it adds the interest of the building (generally, I''ve not seen true sport specific shots place well outside of sport specific challenges).</td>', 'First, head isn''t in a clean spot. Shifting your position slightly left and having him a bit more to the right would help showcase both him and the building. It looks like you missed focus a little bit, as well. Both the concrete stairs, as well as parts of him (especially foot) lack the clarity of the rest of the scene. This may be a situation to consider a hyperfocal setting, upping iso and stopping down another stop, or accepting the lack of clarity in the background, instead (see further down regarding that consideration). From an action sports photo perspective, you want to separate subject from background in a shot like this, but with that cool building, you''re in a tricky spot wherein you need to decide what type of an audience you''re after. Since this is a senior photo shoot, you''re aiming to focus on your model, so I''d say a decrease in DoF would be welcomed. For a photo on here, a deeper DoF will probably work better as it adds the interest of the building (generally, I''ve not seen true sport specific shots place well outside of sport specific challenges).', '2015-04-08 02:27:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(7339306, 1145928, 30982, '<td valign="top">An interesting subject!  As for score, there are a couple of things that might have helped.  The yellow blob above your slug and the black slime on the stick are a bit unappetizing.  Not sure if you could have removed these things or waited a bit for your slug to move?  The composition is just a bit static - if you had your slug more on the diagonal it would feel a bit more dynamic, I think.  Finally, it almost looks as though you used a flash?  The lighting seems just a bit harsh.  If it was a bright day, there''s not much you can do about that, though.  But back to your subject - I don''t think I''ve ever seen one of these in person, and I''m glad you chose to share it with us!  To me, a photograph should share *something* with the viewer, and quite often, the more unique, the better!</td>', 'An interesting subject!  As for score, there are a couple of things that might have helped.  The yellow blob above your slug and the black slime on the stick are a bit unappetizing.  Not sure if you could have removed these things or waited a bit for your slug to move?  The composition is just a bit static - if you had your slug more on the diagonal it would feel a bit more dynamic, I think.  Finally, it almost looks as though you used a flash?  The lighting seems just a bit harsh.  If it was a bright day, there''s not much you can do about that, though.  But back to your subject - I don''t think I''ve ever seen one of these in person, and I''m glad you chose to share it with us!  To me, a photograph should share *something* with the viewer, and quite often, the more unique, the better!', '2015-04-08 02:32:27.000000', NULL, 0),
